`async/await` is syntactic sugar over Promises that makes asynchronous code look synchronous.

```js
async function fetchUser(id) {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  const user = await res.json();
  return user;
}
```

- `async` marks a function as asynchronous — it always returns a Promise.
- `await` pauses execution inside the function until the Promise resolves.
- Wrap in `try/catch` to handle errors.

Use `Promise.all()` to run them in parallel instead of one after another:

```js
// Sequential (slow — waits for each)
const a = await fetchA();
const b = await fetchB();

// Parallel (fast — both start at once)
const [a, b] = await Promise.all([fetchA(), fetchB()]);
```

`Promise.all` rejects immediately if any promise fails. Use `Promise.allSettled` if you want all results regardless of failures.

`Promise.allSettled` is useful when you want to run multiple independent operations and handle each result separately — even if some fail.

```js
const results = await Promise.allSettled([
  fetch('/api/users'),
  fetch('/api/posts'),
  fetch('/api/comments'),
]);

results.forEach(result => {
  if (result.status === 'fulfilled') {
    console.log('Success:', result.value);
  } else {
    console.error('Failed:', result.reason);
  }
});
```

Perfect for dashboards where partial data is still useful.

Absolutely — and you should. A common pattern:

```css
/* Outer page layout with Grid */
.page {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 250px 1fr;
  grid-template-rows: 60px 1fr;
}

/* Inner nav items with Flexbox */
.navbar {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 1rem;
}
```

Grid for the macro layout, Flexbox for the micro components inside it. They complement each other perfectly.
